guide='''<div class="document-heading"><p class="ey">STUDENT & INSTRUCTOR GUIDE</p><h1>One familiar rhythm.<br><em>A new engineering challenge.</em></h1><p class="intro">The original concepts stay visible. iSCARB supplies the reasoning practice around them.</p></div><div class="document-grid"><section class="panel" id="students"><h2>For students</h2><ol class="steps"><li><b>Open the chapter route.</b> Read the five objectives and the fictional case. The classroom sequence includes the original foundations and three short interactions.</li><li><b>Try before revealing.</b> Answer the two short checks and the application task in your own words. Open the example explanation afterward.</li><li><b>Use the source map.</b> A topic studied after class remains required. Each topic links to its classroom location and the original source slides.</li><li><b>Complete the separate assignment.</b> Keep the initial judgment, commit it, inspect STRESS, then justify RETAIN, REVISE or REPLACE.</li><li><b>Review and submit.</b> Declare AI assistance, check every claim, export your work and submit through the LMS.</li></ol></section><section class="panel" id="instructors"><h2>For instructors</h2><p>Begin with the chapter goal and case, then teach the source concepts in order. Use only three planned interaction stops: Check 1, Check 2 and Apply it. Per-page questions can serve self-study without becoming another in-class task.</p><p>For chapters allocated two weeks in the supplied syllabus, use two blocks and pause after Check 2. For one-week chapters, plan 75–90 minutes of explanation and 20–30 minutes of targeted source review. These are estimates: preserve your actual timetable.</p><p>If a required concept is unfinished, schedule it for the next block or explicitly assign its source review. Do not treat the toolkit as a second compulsory lecture.</p><p>Use <b>N</b> for the current unit’s teaching notes. Use the source map to record what remains to be taught in your LMS. The website records material availability, not attendance or teaching completion.</p></section></div><section class="section"><h2>Three places, three purposes</h2><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>Purpose</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Classroom</td><td>Ordered foundations and a bounded application</td><td>Follow the sequence. It ends before the toolkit.</td></tr><tr><td>Source review</td><td>Required original concepts, examples, tables and full source downloads</td><td>Review assigned topics and any unfinished classroom material.</td></tr><tr><td>iSCARB toolkit</td><td>Full Decision Card, six H-Stack capabilities, AI gate, extra retrieval, peer review and deeper examples</td><td>Use a tool when it helps the chapter task. Official assessment scope follows the LMS.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class="section"><h2>Controls and saved work</h2><p>Use Next/Back or the arrow keys. The Units menu separates the classroom route, source review and toolkit. A−/A+ change reading size. Card opens the practice artifact; Download saves the standalone lecture. Source map links and unit URLs let you return to a specific concept.</p><p>Lecture work is local to this browser. Assignment drafts are separated by chapter and Student ID. Neither is automatically sent to the instructor. Download a backup before leaving a shared device. The assignment’s commit sequence preserves your first answer for teaching purposes; it is not a secure examination system.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>What stays consistent</h2><p>CRISIS → MAP → TRADE-OFF → EVIDENCE → VERDICT remains the reasoning thread. The six-field Decision Card remains available. The assignments keep FIT → BOUND → ACT + EVIDENCE → STRESS → REFIT, with one chapter-specific artifact step from Assignment 2 onward.</p><p>Readiness checks support reflection. Filled fields, reviewed pages and practice scores do not certify that an explanation is correct. The instructor judges the reasoning.</p></section>'''
(ROOT/'student-guide.html').write_text(shell('How to study',guide,'guide'))
outcomes=[('Design dependability using redundancy and diversity.','10, 11','Source concepts and design reasoning'),('Write functional and non-functional dependability and security requirements.','10, 11, 13','Requirements and bounded evidence'),('Apply metrics for measurable reliability requirements.','11','Calculation and operational-profile interpretation'),('Design safety in safety-critical systems.','12','Hazard trace and safety case'),('Design and test secure systems.','13','Misuse cases and proposed negative tests'),('Design systems to support resilience.','14','Critical service and recovery reasoning'),('Identify reuse elements and approaches.','15','Reuse strategy comparison'),('Describe composition of standardized reusable components.','16','Interface contract and composition'),('Explain distributed systems and architectures.','17','Architecture and remote-failure reasoning'),('Explain systems of systems and engineering challenges.','20','Governance and staged acceptance'),('Design and implement test cases across testing strategies.','Partial: 11, 12, 13, 16, 17','Test design is practised. Executable test implementation and the separate Code Coverage topic are not established by this release.')]
info='''<div class="document-heading"><p class="ey">COURSE & SOURCES</p><h1>Know the scope.<br><em>Find the evidence.</em></h1><p class="intro">Nine published chapters, one source ledger per chapter, and a visible course-level gap.</p></div><section class="panel"><h2>CPIT-455 · Software Engineering II</h2><p><b>Instructor:</b> Professor Adeeb Noor · King Abdulaziz University<br><b>Prerequisite:</b> CPIT-251<br><b>Primary text:</b> Ian Sommerville, <i>Software Engineering</i>, 10th edition.</p><p>The supplied syllabus file is labelled Fall 2024 and lists 11 course learning outcomes. Its topic allocation guides the learning-path organization. This website does not declare a replacement syllabus: follow the current LMS for grades, deadlines, rooms and the official timetable.</p><p>Each chapter has five local learning objectives. They support the course outcomes below; they do not replace or renumber them.</p></section><section class="section" id="outcomes"><h2>Course-outcome map</h2><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Supplied CLO</th><th>Outcome (shortened)</th><th>Chapter</th><th>Scope in this release</th></tr></thead><tbody>'''
for i,(o,chap,scope) in enumerate(outcomes,1):info+=f'<tr><td>{i}</td><td>{o}</td><td>{chap}</td><td>{scope}</td></tr>'
info+='</tbody></table></div></section><section class="section"><h2>Original source packs</h2><p>Source-slide numbering refers to the supplied pack, not textbook page numbers. Each HTML includes a source text/table ledger. Download the original file for complete slide layout and vector diagrams.</p><div class="source-downloads">'
for n,c in C.items():info+=f'<a href="{counts[n]["source_download"]}"><b>Chapter {n}</b> {c["title"]}<span>{counts[n]["source_slide_count"]} slides · {Path(counts[n]["source_download"]).suffix[1:].upper()} ↓</span></a>'
info+='''</div></section><section class="section"><h2>Assignments and assessment</h2><p>Assignment 1 uses the existing 4-point decision rubric. Assignment 2 retains its 5-point measurement rubric. Assignments 3–9 use five criteria and four performance levels (1, 0.75, 0.5, 0 per criterion), with a chapter-specific artifact. These task points are not course-grade percentages.</p><p>The core workflow remains stable while the artifact progresses: measure, trace, test, recover, compare, specify a contract, reason about remote failure, and integrate across owners. Each assessed case is fictional; any additional evidence must be marked proposed or unknown.</p></section><section class="section"><h2>NCAAA, Jaheziah and iSCARB</h2><p>Knowledge, skills and accountable judgment are made visible through objectives, practice and artifacts. The course files do not establish a current approved national item-weight or departmental mapping. Accordingly, this release does not claim accreditation, official test equivalence or guaranteed national-test readiness.</p><p>The full iSCARB toolkit preserves the six engineering capabilities, Decision Card, AI verification gate and practice explanations. These remain tools to support learning rather than a second set of chapter content to race through.</p></section>'''
(ROOT/'course-resources.html').write_text(shell('Course and sources',info,'info'))
